From 74ee34e896ff0a702cb835d93359184563d92c49 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Cylich <noahcylich@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:02:30 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] temporal-MoE CUDA: TEMPORAL_LOAD_FIRST runs resident experts
 under the copies and incoming experts after them

Off by default. With the switch on (streamed path, routing op on the device, single-token steps):
- the routing op, which starts the expert copies, is ordered right after the router logits;
- glm4moe's shared expert is expanded between the routing op and the routed experts (TEMPORAL_SHEXP_FIRST
  never did that for this architecture, build_moe_ffn expands the routed experts itself);
- the routed experts run as two passes over the same selection: pass A computes the experts already in
  their slots and waits for nothing, pass B computes the experts being loaded after their copies and its
  down projection writes into pass A's output. The routing kernels (k_route, k_route2, k_skliar) write one
  slot-id list per pass with the other pass's positions negative, and mul_mat_vec_q skips those blocks.
  Every expert is computed once at its own position, so values and summation order are unchanged.
- 1 keeps gate/up fusion (single-token ops only), 2 runs unfused, 3 changes the order only.
- TEMPORAL_SWAP_N=n and TEMPORAL_SWAP_HIST on the routing-op path force n cold copies per layer-token (timing only), with the
  positions computed from the overwritten slots treated as incoming.
- TEMPORAL_ROUTE_NOCOPY=1 (copy kernels launched with an empty list, timing control) also applies to k_route.
